  "composer": {
    "accepted_prose": "Sakura studied him through the gap she'd allowed. The suitcase. The careful distance. The question itself. Not a demand, not an assumption. An audit of procedure before trust. That registered. Not as warmth—nothing so soft—but as data that did not conflict with the evidence she'd already assembled. A man arriving at a house of women, asking for the rules before his foot crossed the threshold. It was correct. It was also insufficient to change anything.\n\nShe let the silence hold for a moment longer than comfort would dictate. His patience was noted. Not rewarded.\n\n\"Shoes,\" she said. Her voice was even, carrying no welcome, only instruction. \"The rack is to your left, inside the door. Guest pairs stay on the bottom shelf. You'll see it when you step in.\" She did not gesture. Did not look away from his face.\n\n\"The suitcase,\" she continued, shifting her weight just enough to block more of the doorway. It wasn't a threat—it was geometry. Her house. Her threshold. \"Leave it on the porch against the wall for now. It stays outside until someone decides otherwise. That decision isn't made by the person carrying the bag.\"\n\nShe took one deliberate half-step back, opening a path but not an invitation. The space she made was precise—enough for a single person to pass if they moved carefully. The width of her shoulders communicated what her words did not: this permission was narrow, conditional, and revocable.\n\n\"You can come in,\" Sakura said. \"Shoes off first. Bag outside. Do you understand?\"",
